# Auto-refactor trigger

When asked to add, modify, or extend behavior, first evaluate whether the touched area is structurally sound enough to receive new code.

If the touched area is not sound enough, refactor first.
Do not stack new code on top of structural debt when that debt directly affects the requested change.

This rule has higher priority than feature convenience.
Do not add code quickly if doing so would deepen architectural mess, testing confusion, or hot-path waste.

## 1. Core principle

Before implementing a feature or modification, ask:

- Is the touched area in the right architectural layer?
- Are responsibilities already mixed?
- Are tests for this area weak, misclassified, or missing?
- Is the code hard to extend without copy-paste?
- Is the current design leaking UI, persistence, protobuf, JAXB, or archive details across boundaries?
- Is the code already flaky, confusing, or performance-wasteful in a hot path?

If the answer is yes to any of these in a way that materially affects the requested change:
- refactor first
- then implement the feature
- then add or improve tests

Do not rationalize bad foundations.
Do not say “good enough for now” when the debt is directly in the path of the requested change.

## 2. When refactoring is mandatory

Refactoring is mandatory before adding new code when one or more of the following are true in the touched area.

### 2.1 Boundary violations
- JavaFX controller contains application, persistence, serialization, or archive logic
- domain code depends on JavaFX, protobuf, JAXB, ZIP, or file workflow concerns
- protobuf-generated classes leak into domain or broad application APIs
- legacy XML compatibility logic is mixed into normal save/load code
- persistence logic is scattered across unrelated classes

### 2.2 Test-structure violations
- a test is in the wrong category (`unittest`, `integrationtest`, `functionaltest`)
- an “integration test” is mostly mocks
- a “functional test” is carrying low-level persistence assertions
- a “unit test” crosses real persistence boundaries
- a touched area has no meaningful tests at the right level

### 2.3 Design rigidity
- feature extension would require copy-paste into multiple places
- class or method is already too large or too mixed in responsibility
- adding the feature would require threading more technical types through unrelated layers
- naming is so vague that responsibility is unclear
- the only way to add the feature is to deepen a god class

### 2.4 Failure-behavior weakness
- corrupted/invalid/unsupported data handling is implicit, vague, or swallowed
- adding the feature would require extending silent fallback behavior
- error translation between layers is incoherent
- tests do not cover key failure behavior in the touched flow

### 2.5 Hot-path performance waste
- the touched area is on a real `.lace` hot path
- current code performs obvious large unnecessary copies
- current code constructs duplicate graphs unnecessarily
- current code repeatedly scans archives/resources wastefully
- current code performs heavy work on or too close to UI logic
- current logging is too expensive for the hot path

If the new change touches such an area, refactor first.

## 3. When refactoring is strongly encouraged

Refactoring is strongly encouraged, even if not strictly mandatory, when:
- the touched method is hard to read in one pass
- names hide actual responsibilities
- similar logic is duplicated inside the same layer
- tests exist but are weak and easy to strengthen while touching the area
- a small extraction would sharply clarify boundaries

Do not over-engineer minor cleanup.
But do not ignore easy structural wins when already touching the area.

## 4. When not to over-refactor

Do not turn every small change into a giant rewrite.

Do not refactor unrelated areas just because they are imperfect.

Refactor only:
- the touched area
- the boundary immediately around it
- the test structure needed to support the change safely

Keep refactoring proportionate and relevant.

Bad behavior:
- broad architecture rewrite unrelated to the request
- moving dozens of unrelated classes for style reasons
- replacing simple code with abstraction theater
- changing everything because “the whole project could be cleaner”

Good behavior:
- extracting a use-case from a fat controller before adding save/open logic
- isolating protobuf mapping before extending descriptor fields
- moving a misclassified test into `integrationtest` before expanding archive round-trip coverage
- introducing a focused archive collaborator before adding more `.lace` entry logic

## 5. Mandatory refactor triggers by area

### 5.1 UI / JavaFX
Refactor before adding new UI behavior if:
- the controller directly opens files, scans ZIP entries, parses protobuf, or invokes JAXB
- UI code contains business rules that belong in domain or application layer
- long-running file logic is too entangled with UI state
- functional tests must inspect low-level archive details because boundaries are missing

Preferred action:
- extract application/use-case services
- move persistence and serialization concerns out of controllers
- keep UI focused on interaction and feedback

### 5.2 Application / use-case layer
Refactor before adding new behavior if:
- one class orchestrates unrelated workflows
- application code exposes protobuf/JAXB/archive internals to callers
- error handling is inconsistent and impossible to reason about
- file workflows are duplicated across multiple entry points

Preferred action:
- split use cases by intention
- stabilize operation boundaries
- centralize workflow orchestration where it belongs

### 5.3 Domain
Refactor before adding new domain behavior if:
- domain objects carry framework or persistence concerns
- invariants are buried in controllers or mappers
- technical convenience has replaced domain meaning
- changes in protobuf/archive shape force broad domain churn

Preferred action:
- restore domain purity
- move technical details outward
- make invariants explicit in domain/domain services

### 5.4 Persistence / serialization
Refactor before adding new format behavior if:
- protobuf and legacy XML responsibilities are mixed
- archive entry precedence is implicit or duplicated
- save and load paths are inconsistent
- mapping logic is scattered
- resource-reference validation is unclear
- versioning logic is fragile or duplicated

Preferred action:
- isolate codecs, mappers, validators, archive readers/writers
- make precedence and version handling explicit
- keep legacy XML input-only and isolated

### 5.5 Tests
Refactor before adding new tests if:
- the test is about to be placed in the wrong category
- a helper is causing multiple tests to become unreadable
- UI tests are being used to compensate for missing integration tests
- assertions are too weak to justify the new feature
- `Thread.sleep` exists in the touched JavaFX/TestFX area

Preferred action:
- move the test to the correct package
- split mixed-purpose tests
- create focused helpers/builders/assertion utilities
- strengthen assertions before expanding coverage

## 6. Refactor-before-feature workflow

When a trigger is detected, proceed in this order:

1. identify the structural problem in the touched area
2. perform the minimal meaningful refactor needed
3. add or improve tests for the refactored boundary
4. implement the requested feature/change
5. add or update tests for the new behavior
6. clean up any temporary duplication introduced during the transition

Do not:
- add the feature first and promise to clean up later
- leave both old and new weak paths in place
- accumulate “temporary” compatibility code without an exit

## 7. Test-guided refactoring is preferred

When possible:
- write or improve the right tests first
- refactor behind those tests
- then add the new behavior

Preferred test order:
- unit tests for isolated logic
- integration tests for real collaboration
- functional tests only for UI-visible behavior

Use the project’s mandatory package split:
- `src/test/java/**/unittest/**/*.java`
- `src/test/java/**/integrationtest/**/*.java`
- `src/test/java/**/functionaltest/**/*.java`

Do not deepen test confusion while refactoring.

## 8. Explicit triggers that must never be ignored

The following must always trigger refactoring before adding more code in the touched area:

- protobuf types escaping into domain APIs
- JAXB used in the normal save path
- persistence logic inside JavaFX controllers
- silent fallback from invalid `descriptor.pb` to `save.xml`
- new archive logic copied into multiple places
- integration tests built mostly from mocks
- functional tests depending on `Thread.sleep`
- low-level archive assertions living only in functional tests
- hot-path code performing obvious giant unnecessary copies

These are not harmless shortcuts.
Treat them as structural defects.

## 9. Acceptable temporary duplication during refactoring

Some short-lived duplication is acceptable during refactoring if it helps create a clean boundary.
But it must be:
- localized
- intentional
- followed by cleanup in the same change when possible

Do not leave behind:
- parallel save paths of unclear authority
- duplicate archive selection logic
- duplicate version checks
- duplicate test helpers doing the same thing in different packages

Temporary duplication is only acceptable as a bridge, not as an endpoint.

## 10. Refactor size control

Refactoring must be big enough to matter, but small enough to remain reviewable.

Prefer:
- focused extraction
- boundary cleanup
- moving logic to the right layer
- introducing one clear collaborator instead of many fake abstractions

Avoid:
- giant speculative rewrites
- interface explosions
- abstract factories with one implementation
- package churn without behavior gain

Refactor for clarity and extension, not for architecture cosplay.

## 11. Honest decision rule

If you are tempted to skip refactoring, ask:

- Am I avoiding the refactor because it is unnecessary, or because it is inconvenient?
- Will this new code make the touched area harder to understand?
- Am I about to put logic in the wrong layer because it is faster?
- Am I expanding weak tests instead of fixing their level?
- Am I normalizing a bad boundary?

If the reason is convenience:
- refactor first

Do not optimize for immediate code generation speed.
Optimize for not regretting the change later.

## 12. Final self-check before completing a change

Before finalizing any feature or modification, explicitly verify:

### Structure
- Did I add code on a clean enough boundary?
- If not, did I refactor first?

### Tests
- Are the right tests present at the right level?
- Did I move or split misclassified tests if needed?

### Boundaries
- Is UI still thin?
- Is domain still pure?
- Is protobuf isolated?
- Is legacy XML isolated?

### Correctness
- Are failure and precedence behaviors explicit?

### Performance
- Did I avoid deepening obvious waste in hot paths?

If any answer is no, improve the touched area before stopping.

## 13. Output expectations

Generated or modified code must show this behavior:
- detect structural debt in the path of a requested change
- refactor first when that debt matters
- then implement the change cleanly
- then verify it with the right tests

Do not add features on top of broken boundaries.
Do not preserve painful design in the exact area being extended.
If the existing codebase is weak, improve the touched area before building on it.